# T-3068 — the trigger for PATH A of the wake rail.
# Installed to: /etc/cron.d/termlink-notify-wake-supervisor
#
# Keeps one notify-wake-consumer alive per agent in
# .context/cron/notify-wake-agents.conf, so a raised notify flag is actually READ.
#
# WHY A SUPERVISOR AND NOT THE CONSUMER DIRECTLY. The consumer runs --follow, a
# long-lived poll loop. Cron cannot keep such a process alive; it can only re-check
# that one exists. So cron runs the SUPERVISOR every 5 minutes and the supervisor
# restarts anything missing. Worst case after a crash or reboot: 5 minutes dark,
# bounded and known, rather than dark until someone notices — which is how the rail
# managed 82 days (G-069).
#
# The redirect is SPLIT on purpose (T-2685). Merging stderr into the findings log
# would let a tooling error dirty the one-bit "empty log = healthy" channel, after
# which a genuine finding appends to an already-dirty file and changes nothing an
# operator can see. `2>/dev/null` is equally wrong: it trades a false positive for
# a silent failure, which is Directive #2 violated inside the monitoring layer.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/root/.cargo/bin

*/5 * * * * root cd /opt/termlink && bash scripts/notify-wake-supervisor.sh --quiet >> .context/working/.notify-wake-supervisor.log 2>> .context/working/.notify-wake-supervisor.log.stderr
